cartridge "ARCADE BASEBALL/Kevin Mykytyn and Mark Tuttle/1986"

' ARCADE BASEBALL
' A ColecoVision game based on "Arcade Baseball", a type-in from COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette, May 1986 (Commodore 64, BASIC with a machine-language pitch and
' swing routine). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic),
' 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a cell-for-cell port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' BASIC listing and a disassembly of its machine code, orig/), the game is
' built for the ColecoVision: smooth sprites, a tiled diamond, runners who
' run the bases, sound effects and an original title tune.
'
' The game: the pitcher throws one of four pitches; press the button to
' swing. One swing a pitch. Where the bat meets the ball sends it left,
' centre or right: early swings pull it to the left, late ones push it
' right. The ball flies to the row of zones along the top: 1B, OUT, 2B, OUT,
' 3B, OUT, 2B, OUT, 1B (a fielder stands in each OUT zone). A ball hit
' straight back over the pitcher's mound is a home run. A miss is a strike;
' three strikes are an out; three outs end the half inning. On a hit every
' runner (and the batter) moves up as many bases as the hit is worth; a
' runner who reaches home scores a run.
'   PRACTICE: 20 pitches from the computer, nothing counted.
'   1 PLAYER: one inning (three outs) against the computer's pitching: how
'   many runs can you score?
'   2 PLAYERS: three innings, the visitor (controller 1) bats first while
'   the home player (controller 2) pitches, then they change; tied after
'   three innings, extra innings until one team leads after a full inning.
'
' From the original (orig/arcade baseball.bas, orig/ml.bin): the zones and
' their order (table at $C0AF: 1, out, 2, out, 3, out, 2, out, 1); the home
' run when the ball passes the pitcher's mound line within a few pixels of
' the middle ($C2FE); the 28 hit directions by contact offset (table at
' $C316, resampled to 25 here) and the hit's climb; the four pitches (curve
' right, fastball, slow ball, curve left: speeds $C8, $FA, $B4, $C8 and
' curves +5, 0, 0, -8 at $C1CA-$C1D5), relative speeds kept; the computer
' pitches after 64-127 frames ($C155) with a random pitch; bouncing off the
' side walls; every miss a strike (no balls); runners advance by the hit's
' bases, all of them on a home run; three strikes, three outs, the inning
' count, 3 innings for two players with extra innings on a tie, one inning
' for one player, 20 practice pitches.
'
' Liberties: the speeds are slowed down for human reactions (the fastball
' takes about 0.7 s from the mound to the plate) and the pitcher winds up
' visibly before every pitch. The visitor bats first (the listing started
' with the home team). In two-player games the pitcher aims with the stick
' and throws with a button (C64: the F1/F3/F5/F7 keys): up or centre
' fastball, down slow ball, left/right the curves. The ball lands in the
' zone under it (on the C64 it drifted sideways when it came down on a post
' between two zones). Runners run the bases instead of jumping; a result
' message replaces the border flash of a home run. Practice shows the
' result of each hit.
'
' Controls: joystick and side buttons only. Button: swing. Pitcher (2
' players): stick for the pitch, button to throw. On the title, up and down
' choose, a button starts (or opens HOW TO PLAY, three pages: a button
' turns the page, 30 s idle goes back; or CREDITS).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Own xorshift random numbers. Nothing flashes.

